This function creates a new `CorrectiveSmoothModifier`, assigns it to the target object's data block (specifically for mesh objects), sets its vertex group reference, selects the "Vertex" smoothing mode if not inverted, applies an initial strength of 0.5, and returns the modifier instance so further configuration can occur.

```python
def add_corrective_smooth(obj: bpy.types.Object, name: str, vgroup: str, only_smooth: bool, invert: bool) -> bpy.types.CorrectiveSmoothModifier:
    if not obj.data_type == 'MESH':
        raise ValueError("Corrective Smooth modifier can only be added to mesh objects.")

    ms = obj.modifiers.new(name=name, type='CORRECTIVE_SMOOTH')

    # Set vertex group reference (assumes the object has a matching named vertex group)
    if vgroup in [vg.name for vg in obj.vertex_groups]:
        ms.group_index = obj.vertex_groups.index(vgroup) + 1

    # Determine smoothing mode based on inversion flag and only_smooth setting
    if invert:
        ms.smooth_mode = 'NORMAL'
    elif only_smooth:
        ms.smooth_mode = 'VERTEX'
    else:
        ms.smooth_mode = 'MIXED'

    # Set initial strength to a reasonable default for testing/visualization
    ms.strength = 0.5

    return ms
```

